{"meta":{"title":"Equilibrium Conditions","chapter_title":"Equilibrium of Particles","course":"statics","chapter":"01","section":"01","estimated_time_minutes":28,"learning_objectives":[{"id":"statics-01-01-lo-01","statement":"Define equilibrium for a particle"},{"id":"statics-01-01-lo-02","statement":"Apply the condition ΣF = 0 to solve for unknown forces"},{"id":"statics-01-01-lo-03","statement":"Construct and interpret free body diagrams"},{"id":"statics-01-01-lo-04","statement":"Calculate tension and reaction forces in particle-equilibrium problems"}],"concept_ids":["equilibrium","sum_of_forces","free_body_diagram","tension"],"prereq_section_ids":[],"practice_ids":["statics_eq_vertical_tension","statics_eq_inclined_tension","statics_eq_component_rule","statics_eq_second_vertical","statics_01_01_lo_03_direct_check"],"lo_practice_map":{"statics-01-01-lo-01":["statics_eq_component_rule"],"statics-01-01-lo-02":["statics_eq_component_rule"],"statics-01-01-lo-03":["statics_01_01_lo_03_direct_check"],"statics-01-01-lo-04":["statics_eq_component_rule","statics_eq_vertical_tension","statics_eq_inclined_tension","statics_eq_second_vertical"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Equilibrium Conditions\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Concept relationship map connecting Equilibrium, Sum Of Forces, and Free Body Diagram within Equilibrium Conditions.\" conceptid=\"equilibrium\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/reference-figures/statics/01-01-equilibrium-conditions.png\" alt=\"Equilibrium Conditions concept map with Equilibrium, Sum Of Forces, and Free Body Diagram connected to the section focus.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n\n## Start Here: Force Balance\n\nA suspended light, a cable-supported sensor, and a wall bracket are different systems with the same first test: **do all external forces balance?** Equilibrium analysis turns that physical question into a reliable model.\n\n**In this section:** define particle equilibrium, apply $\\sum \\vec{F}=0$, construct free body diagrams, and solve for basic tension and reaction forces.\n\n> **Load-path check:** A balanced equation is meaningful only when every force in the model has a physical source and a defensible direction.\n\n## What Is Equilibrium?\n\n### The Physical Concept\n\nImagine a book resting on a table. It does not accelerate because the downward weight and upward table reaction balance exactly.\n\nThis is **equilibrium**: a state where the net effect of all forces is zero.\n\n| State | Condition | Physical Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Static Equilibrium | $\\sum \\vec{F} = 0$ and $v = 0$ | Object at rest, stays at rest |\n| Dynamic Equilibrium | $\\sum \\vec{F} = 0$ and $v = \\text{const}$ | Object moves at constant velocity |\n\n> **Key Insight:** Equilibrium does not mean \"no forces.\" It means the forces balance.\n\n### The Particle Model\n\nIn this chapter, we model objects as **particles** - idealized bodies where all forces act at a single point.\n\nThat simplification is useful when:\n\n- the object's size is small relative to the geometry of interest\n- rotational effects can be ignored\n- the goal is to study translational balance only\n\nLater chapters will extend this idea to rigid bodies, where moment balance matters too.\n\n---\n\n## The Equilibrium Condition\n\n### Vector Form\n\nFor a particle in equilibrium,\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\sum \\vec{F} = 0}\n$$\n\nThis is Newton's first law written in statics language.\n\n### Component Form\n\nIn 2D problems, engineers usually split the vector equation into components:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0\n$$\n\nThese are algebraic sums. Forces acting opposite the chosen positive direction must carry a negative sign.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A particle has only two forces on it: a 40 N pull to the right and a 40 N pull to the left. The particle is at rest. What does the equilibrium condition tell you about its state?\" explanation=\"Both components cancel: the sum of x-forces is +40 - 40 = 0 and there are no y-forces, so the net force is zero. Equilibrium does not require zero forces, only zero net force, so the particle stays at rest.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is in equilibrium because the net force is zero even though two real forces act.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is not in equilibrium because two nonzero forces are present.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It must be accelerating because forces are acting on it.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n> **Critical Check:** Most statics mistakes are not advanced mistakes. They are usually sign mistakes, missing forces, or sloppy free body diagrams.\n\n---\n\n## Forces in Equilibrium Problems\n\nBefore solving, identify **all** external forces on the particle.\n\n| Force Type | Symbol | Direction | When It Appears |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Weight | $W$ or $mg$ | Always downward | Any object with mass |\n| Tension | $T$ | Along the cable, away from the object | Ropes, strings, cables |\n| Normal Force | $N$ | Perpendicular to the surface | Contact with a surface |\n| Friction | $f$ | Parallel to the surface | Rough contact surfaces |\n| Applied Force | $P$ or $F$ | Given by the problem | External loading |\n\n> **Golden Rule for Tension:** Cables can only pull, never push. Tension always acts away from the object along the cable direction.\n\n---\n\n## Free Body Diagrams: The Master Skill\n\nThe **Free Body Diagram (FBD)** is the central skill in introductory statics. A correct FBD does not guarantee a correct answer, but an incorrect FBD almost guarantees a wrong one.\n\n### Six-Step FBD Procedure\n\n1. **Isolate** the body of interest\n2. **Choose** a clear coordinate system\n3. **Draw** weight vertically downward\n4. **Add** cable, contact, or support forces in the correct directions\n5. **Label** unknowns symbolically before solving\n6. **Check** whether every force is external to the isolated body\n\n### Why This Step Matters\n\nA free body diagram is not just a sketch. It is the modeling step that translates a physical situation into solvable equations.\n\nGood analysts pause here and ask:\n\n- Did I isolate the right object?\n- Did I accidentally include an internal force?\n- Does each force have a physical source?\n- Does each assumed direction make sense?\n\n---\n\n## Worked Examples\n\n### Example 1.1: Vertical Hanging Weight\n\n**Problem:** A 25 kg lamp hangs from a vertical cable. Find the cable tension.\n\n**Step 1: Draw the FBD**\n\nThe lamp has two forces:\n\n- upward cable tension $T$\n- downward weight $W = mg$\n\n**Step 2: Apply Equilibrium**\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nT - W = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nT = W = mg\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Compute the Value**\n\n$$\nT = (25 \\text{ kg})(9.81 \\text{ m/s}^2) = \\boxed{245.25 \\text{ N}}\n$$\n\nThis is the simplest equilibrium pattern: one support force balancing one known load.\n\n---\n\n### Example 1.2: Single Inclined Cable\n\n**Problem:** A 50 kg crate is suspended by a cable at $45^\\circ$ above the horizontal. Find the cable tension.\n\n**Step 1: Resolve the Force**\n\nThe cable tension has components:\n\n| Component | Expression |\n| --- | --- |\n| $T_x$ | $T \\cos 45^\\circ$ |\n| $T_y$ | $T \\sin 45^\\circ$ |\n\nOnly the vertical component balances the weight.\n\n**Step 2: Apply Vertical Equilibrium**\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nT \\sin 45^\\circ - mg = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nT = \\frac{mg}{\\sin 45^\\circ}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Calculate**\n\n$$\nT = \\frac{(50)(9.81)}{0.707} = \\boxed{693.67 \\text{ N}}\n$$\n\nNotice that $T > W$. That is not a mistake. Only part of the cable force acts vertically, so the full tension must be larger than the supported weight.\n\n> **Think About It:** As the cable angle gets closer to horizontal, the required tension increases dramatically. This is one reason shallow cable angles can create very large internal forces.\n\nUse the explorer below to feel that effect directly. Drive the cable angle down toward the horizontal and watch the required tension climb far above the supported weight, while the horizontal pull on the anchor grows even faster.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Single inclined cable: T = mg / sin(theta)\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"m\",\"label\":\"Hanging mass\",\"min\":5,\"max\":200,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"kg\",\"default\":50},{\"name\":\"theta\",\"label\":\"Cable angle above horizontal\",\"min\":5,\"max\":90,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"deg\",\"default\":45}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Weight W = mg\",\"unit\":\"N\",\"expr\":\"m * 9.81\"},{\"label\":\"Cable tension T = mg / sin(theta)\",\"unit\":\"N\",\"expr\":\"m * 9.81 / sin(theta * 3.14159265 / 180)\"},{\"label\":\"Horizontal anchor pull T cos(theta)\",\"unit\":\"N\",\"expr\":\"m * 9.81 * cos(theta * 3.14159265 / 180) / sin(theta * 3.14159265 / 180)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"theta\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n---\n\n### Example 1.3: Two-Cable System\n\n**Problem:** A 100 kg crate is suspended by two cables. Cable A makes $30^\\circ$ with the horizontal and Cable B makes $60^\\circ$ with the horizontal on the opposite side. Find $T_A$ and $T_B$.\n\n**Step 1: Write Horizontal Equilibrium**\n\n$$\nT_A \\cos 30^\\circ - T_B \\cos 60^\\circ = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nT_A (0.866) = T_B (0.5)\n$$\n\n$$\nT_A = 0.577 T_B\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Write Vertical Equilibrium**\n\n$$\nT_A \\sin 30^\\circ + T_B \\sin 60^\\circ - W = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nT_A (0.5) + T_B (0.866) = 981\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Substitute and Solve**\n\n$$\n(0.577 T_B)(0.5) + 0.866 T_B = 981\n$$\n\n$$\n1.155 T_B = 981\n$$\n\n$$\nT_B = \\boxed{849.4 \\text{ N}}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\nT_A = 0.577(849.4) = \\boxed{490.1 \\text{ N}}\n$$\n\nThis result is physically informative. In this geometry, the steeper cable carries the larger force: $T_B = 849.4\\,\\text{N}$ while the shallower cable carries $T_A = 490.1\\,\\text{N}$.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Reconstruct the two-cable solution step by step\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Horizontal equilibrium gives T_A cos30 = T_B cos60. Solving for T_A in terms of T_B, which relation is correct?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"T_A = 1.732 T_B\",\"T_A = 0.577 T_B\",\"T_A = T_B\",\"T_A = 0.5 T_B\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"T_A = T_B (cos60 / cos30) = T_B (0.5 / 0.866) = 0.577 T_B. Therefore cable A carries less tension than cable B for this geometry.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Vertical equilibrium reads T_A sin30 + T_B sin60 = W, with W = (100)(9.81) = 981 N. After substituting T_A = 0.577 T_B, what single equation in T_B results?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"1.155 T_B = 981\",\"0.866 T_B = 981\",\"1.366 T_B = 981\",\"0.577 T_B = 981\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"(0.577 T_B)(0.5) + T_B(0.866) = 0.289 T_B + 0.866 T_B = 1.155 T_B = 981.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Solve for T_B.\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"490.1 N\",\"981 N\",\"849.4 N\",\"566.2 N\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"T_B = 981 / 1.155 = 849.4 N. This is the steeper (60 degree) cable.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Back-substitute: T_A = 0.577 (849.4) = 490.1 N. The shallower 30 degree cable carries LESS force here because it sits closer to the load line, contradicting a common intuition. The closed form T_A = W cos(beta)/sin(alpha+beta) confirms both numbers.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n**Quick generalization.** For two cables at angles $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ measured from the horizontal on opposite sides of a weight $W$, the same two equations solve in closed form:\n\n$$\nT_A = \\frac{W \\cos\\beta}{\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta)}, \\qquad T_B = \\frac{W \\cos\\alpha}{\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha = 30^\\circ$, $\\beta = 60^\\circ$, and $W = 981$ N gives $T_A = 981(0.5)/\\sin 90^\\circ = 490.5$ N and $T_B = 981(0.866)/1 = 849.4$ N, matching the step-by-step result above. The formula also shows why support becomes dangerous as the cables flatten toward each other: as $(\\alpha + \\beta) \\to 0$, $\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta) \\to 0$ and both tensions grow without bound.\n\n---\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\n| Application | Why Equilibrium Matters |\n| --- | --- |\n| Suspension Bridges | Cable forces must balance deck and live loads |\n| Tower Cranes | Counterweights and booms must balance safely |\n| Cable Cars | Support and tension systems must carry cabin loads |\n| Sensor Mounts | Small brackets and cable systems still require force balance |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** The same equilibrium logic used on a hanging lamp also appears in bridge hangers, lifting rigs, antenna stays, and instrument-support assemblies. The scale changes, but the force-balance logic does not.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Equilibrium (vector) | $\\sum \\vec{F} = 0$ |\n| Equilibrium (2D) | $\\sum F_x = 0$, $\\sum F_y = 0$ |\n| Weight | $W = mg$ |\n| Tension Components | $T_x = T \\cos\\theta$, $T_y = T \\sin\\theta$ |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Equilibrium means the net force is zero.\n- The free body diagram is the essential setup tool.\n- Always define a coordinate system before writing equations.\n- Tension acts along the cable, away from the object.\n- A plausible answer should make physical sense in both sign and magnitude.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn **Section 1.2: Free Body Diagrams**, we will extend this force-balance workflow to more complex support conditions, inclined surfaces, and frictional contacts.\n\n---\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"equilibrium\",\"sum_of_forces\",\"free_body_diagram\",\"tension\"]'></concept-map>\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Particle Equilibrium\" context=\"A field engineer must decide whether a suspended sensor package can be safely supported by two angled guide cables after one mounting point is moved during installation.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A particle is in equilibrium under several forces. Which statement must be true?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"The sum of force components in every direction is zero.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Each individual force must be zero.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"The particle cannot be moving at constant velocity.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. Equilibrium means the net force is zero, not that every separate force vanishes.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## References\n\n- OpenStax. (2016). *University Physics, Volume 1.* Chapter 12: Static Equilibrium and Elasticity. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-introduction\n- OpenStax. (2016). *University Physics, Volume 1.* Section 10.6: Torque. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/10-6-torque\n- OpenStax. (2016). *University Physics, Volume 1.* Section 12.1: Conditions for Static Equilibrium. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-1-conditions-for-static-equilibrium\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"statics_eq_vertical_tension","type":"numeric","difficulty":"easy","statement":"A 25 kg lamp hangs vertically from a single cable. What is the tension in the cable?","givens":{"mass":"25 kg","g":"9.81 m/s^2"},"expected_value":245.25,"expected_unit":"N","tolerance":0.05,"require_unit":true,"concept_ids":["equilibrium","tension"],"hint":"For a vertically hanging object in equilibrium, tension equals weight.","learning_objective_ids":["statics-01-01-lo-04"]},{"id":"statics_eq_inclined_tension","type":"numeric","difficulty":"medium","statement":"A 50 kg object is suspended by a cable at 45 degrees from the horizontal. Calculate the tension in the cable.","givens":{"mass":"50 kg","angle":"45 deg","g":"9.81 m/s^2"},"expected_value":693.67,"expected_unit":"N","tolerance":0.1,"require_unit":true,"concept_ids":["equilibrium","tension","force_components"],"hint":"Use vertical equilibrium: only the vertical component of the cable tension supports the weight.","learning_objective_ids":["statics-01-01-lo-04"]},{"id":"statics_eq_component_rule","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"easy","stem":"For a particle in 2D equilibrium, which equations must both be satisfied?","options":["Only sum M = 0","sum Fx = 0 and sum Fy = 0","T = W in every problem","Only the largest force matters"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"In planar particle equilibrium, the net force must be zero in both coordinate directions.","concept_ids":["equilibrium","sum_of_forces"],"misconception_id":"tension_equals_weight_always","learning_objective_ids":["statics-01-01-lo-01","statics-01-01-lo-02","statics-01-01-lo-04"]},{"id":"statics_eq_second_vertical","type":"numeric","difficulty":"easy","statement":"A 12 kg sign hangs at rest from a vertical cable. Calculate the cable tension.","givens":{"mass":"12 kg","g":"9.81 m/s^2"},"expected_value":117.72,"expected_unit":"N","tolerance":0.05,"require_unit":true,"concept_ids":["equilibrium","tension"],"hint":"For a hanging body at rest, cable tension balances the weight.","learning_objective_ids":["statics-01-01-lo-04"]},{"id":"statics_01_01_lo_03_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Construct and interpret free body diagrams.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Construct and interpret free body diagrams, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["statics-01-01-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"equilibrium_means_no_forces","pattern":"concept_mixup","description":"Assuming equilibrium means there are no forces acting at all","trigger":"learner says a body in equilibrium has zero forces on it","feedback":"Equilibrium means the vector sum of forces is zero. Individual forces can still be large as long as they balance each other.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"tension_equals_weight_always","pattern":"formula_selection","description":"Assuming cable tension always equals weight even for inclined cables","trigger":"learner sets T = mg without resolving components for an angled cable","feedback":"For an inclined cable, only the vertical component of tension balances weight. The full tension is usually larger than mg.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"sign_error_force_balance","pattern":"sign_error","description":"Losing track of positive and negative directions in force sums","trigger":"learner adds all force magnitudes as positive values","feedback":"Equilibrium equations are algebraic sums. Forces opposite the positive axis must carry negative signs.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"3cb5abe86d6ef480daf801ee483463243f6150b3819454ad393878bd8616c908","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}